.TH MSTFLINTLOGGER "1" "September 2026" "mstflint" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mstflintlogger \- configure the NVIDIA Tools logger used by the mstflint tools
.SH DESCRIPTION
NAME
.IP
mstflintlogger
.PP
SYNOPSIS
.IP
mstflintlogger [OPTIONS]
.IP
[\-l|\-\-set\-level <LEVEL>] [\-m|\-\-set\-module <MODULE:LEVEL>]
[\-M|\-\-clear\-module <MODULE>] [\-o|\-\-enable\-output <SINK>]
[\-O|\-\-disable\-output <SINK>] [\-n|\-\-set\-max\-log\-files <COUNT>]
[\-s|\-\-show] [\-r|\-\-reset] [\-c|\-\-config\-file <PATH>] [\-h|\-\-help]
[\-v|\-\-version]
.PP
DESCRIPTION
.IP
mstflintlogger configures the NVIDIA Tools logger used by the mstflint tools. It reads
and writes a single JSON file, /var/lib/mstflint/mstflintlogger.json, which every tool
loads once at process start. Verbosity is therefore data, not code: no tool
flag, no environment variable and no rebuild is involved, and one command
changes what every tool logs.
.IP
Logging is off until it is turned on. With no configuration file present, no
output is produced at all.
.IP
A configuration change affects processes started afterwards; a tool that is
already running is unaffected.
.IP
Severity levels are debug, info, warning, error, fatal and off. A level means
"this level and above"; off silences the layer. Output sinks are stdout,
stderr, file and syslog. The file sink writes
/var/log/mstflint/<executable>_<pid>.log.
.IP
Writing the default configuration file requires root. Use \-\-config\-file to
work on an unprivileged copy.
.PP
OPTIONS
.IP
mstflintlogger [OPTIONS]
.TP
\fB\-l\fR|\-\-set\-level <LEVEL>
: Set the global severity threshold, used by every layer with no override
.TP
\fB\-m\fR|\-\-set\-module <MODULE:LEVEL>
: Set a per\-layer severity override, e.g. mtcr:debug. May be repeated. Use
all:<LEVEL> to set every layer
.TP
\fB\-M\fR|\-\-clear\-module <MODULE>
: Drop a per\-layer override so the layer follows the global level. May be
repeated. Use all to drop every override
.TP
\fB\-o\fR|\-\-enable\-output <SINK>
: Add an output sink. May be repeated
.TP
\fB\-O\fR|\-\-disable\-output <SINK>
: Remove an output sink. May be repeated
.TP
\fB\-n\fR|\-\-set\-max\-log\-files <COUNT>
: Cap the number of .log files kept in the log directory (default: 100). Older
files beyond the cap are deleted when a new log file is opened
.TP
\fB\-s\fR|\-\-show
: Print the configuration and the resolved severity of every layer, tagged
(override) or (global)
.TP
\fB\-r\fR|\-\-reset
: Reset the configuration to defaults, i.e. logging off
.TP
\fB\-c\fR|\-\-config\-file <PATH>
: Operate on a different configuration file (default:
/var/lib/mstflint/mstflintlogger.json)
.TP
\fB\-h\fR|\-\-help
: Show help message and exit
.TP
\fB\-v\fR|\-\-version
: Show version and exit
.PP
LAYERS
.IP
A layer is the unit of on/off control: one name in the log record, one key in
the configuration file, one argument to \-\-set\-module. The layers are:
.IP
mtcr, reg_access, flint, mlxconfig, mlxlink, mlxreg, mft_core, mlxfwops, mst_tool,
efuse, common, hca_caps
.IP
all is not a layer, it is a keyword accepted by \-\-set\-module and
\-\-clear\-module that expands to every layer.
.PP
NOTES
.IP
Clears are applied before sets, so \-\-clear\-module all \-\-set\-module
flint:debug in a single command means "drop every override, then enable just
flint".
.IP
\-\-show combined with a modification flag does not print; run \-\-show on its
own to inspect.
.IP
The configuration file is replaced atomically, so a tool starting while it is
being written sees either the old contents or the new, never a partial file.
.PP
EXAMPLES
.IP
Log the low\-level device access layer to a file, reproduce a failure, then put
things back:
.IP
# mstflintlogger \-\-enable\-output file \-\-set\-module mtcr:debug
.IP
# mstflintlogger \-\-show
.IP
# mstflint \-d /dev/mst/mt4123_pciconf0 q
.IP
# less /var/log/mstflint/mstflint_<pid>.log
.IP
# mstflintlogger \-\-clear\-module mtcr \-\-disable\-output file
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B mstflint(1)
